# Logic and constraints

```elixir
System.put_env("AL_MNESIA_DISTRIBUTED", "false")

System.put_env(
  "AL_MNESIA_DIR",
  Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "al_livebook_#{:erlang.unique_integer([:positive])}")
)

Mix.install(
  [{:al, github: "anoma/AL-Ex"}],
  config: [al: [transaction_programs: [:bootstrap], natives: []]]
)
```

## Setup

This notebook is self-contained. The cell above installs AL directly, no separate node, no cookie, nothing to attach to. The first run compiles AL and its dependencies from scratch, which takes a minute or two. Reopening this notebook later is fast, since `Mix.install` caches by the exact dependency and config combination. State here is throwaway. It lives only for this notebook's session and disappears when the session ends.

If you already have a real AL node running and want the persistent, shared store instead, see the [intro](./intro.livemd) for how to attach to it instead.

`Mix.install` has to finish before `use AL` loads the library, so that happens in its own cell here.

```elixir
use AL

AL.Branch.head()
```

## Run a method backwards

```elixir
run(~S"""
  factorial N 120.
  """)
```

## `=`: a real constraint

`=` doesn't care which side is open. It's a real constraint, checked at bind time like `dif`, not a one-shot evaluation.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  = N 5.
  = N1 (- N 1).
  """)
```

The var can just as easily be buried inside the expression, on either side. No separate mode is needed.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  = X 10.
  = X (+ Y 3).
  """)
```

## Propagate constraints about and between objects

```elixir
run(~S"""
  @rectangle
  #{super => value, ivars => [#{name => width}, #{name => height}, #{name => perimeter}]}.

  rectangle >> init
  | Self Args New |
  get Args width W,
  get Args height H,
  get Args perimeter P,
  = P (+ (* 2 W) (* 2 H)),
  = New #{class => rectangle, height => H, perimeter => P, width => W}.

  rectangle >> area
  | Self Result |
  get Self width W,
  get Self height H,
  = Result (* W H).

  new rectangle #{height => 3, perimeter => 16, width => W} R.
  area R A.
  """)
```

## Comparisons narrow instead of failing

`< > <= >=` don't need a ground operand. An open var just gets its interval narrowed instead.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  > Y 1.
  """)
```

`Y` is still open here, just narrowed rather than bound to one value. Only a genuinely non-numeric ground operand still hard-fails, since there's no interval to narrow against it.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  > Y not_a_number.
  """)
```

## Posting order doesn't matter

Posting `< X Y` alone narrows nothing observable, since both sides are still open. But once `< Y 5` is posted too, `X` is transitively bound to satisfy the whole chain, without `< X Y` ever being re-checked by hand. This is the propagator sitting parked and re-firing on its own, not a one-shot check.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  < X Y.
  < Y 5.
  = X 2.
  """)
```

```elixir
run(~S"""
  < X Y.
  < Y 5.
  = X 10.
  """)
```

The second one fails: `10` can't be less than something itself less than `5`. Nothing about the order you posted the constraints in mattered, only what they jointly imply.

## Infer an object's identity from its class and a slot

An open receiver turns the last two calls below into a search, not a lookup.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  @direct_vehicle
  #{super => object}.

  @direct_car
  #{super => direct_vehicle, ivars => [#{default => red, name => color}]}.

  @direct_hydrant
  #{super => object, ivars => [#{default => red, name => color}]}.

  new direct_car Car.
  new direct_hydrant Hydrant.
  findall X Direct {class X direct_vehicle, label X, get X color red}.
  findall X Inherited {isa X direct_vehicle, label X, get X color red}.
  findall X ConstrainedFirst {isa X direct_vehicle, get X color red, label X}.
  class Car direct_car.
  not (class Car direct_vehicle).
  isa Car direct_vehicle.
  isa Candidate Ancestor.
  = Ancestor direct_vehicle.
  = Candidate Car.
  """, branch: Examples.Support.branch())
```

## `dif` and `all_dif`

`dif A B` never resolves by binding anything itself. It only rules out bindings that would make its two sides equal.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  dif X 1.
  = X 2.
  """)
```

```elixir
run(~S"""
  dif X 1.
  = X 1.
  """)
```

Posted on a still-open var, `dif` drives backtracking directly. `member`'s first clause offers `= X 1`, which violates the constraint and fails, so it falls through to `= X 2` without `1` ever surfacing as a candidate.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  dif X 1.
  member [1, 2, 3] X.
  """)
```

`all_dif` is the same idea over a whole list at once: every pair has to satisfy `dif`.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  all_dif [1, 2, 3].
  """)
```

```elixir
run(~S"""
  all_dif [1, 2, 1].
  """)
```

Combined with `in_domain` below, `all_dif` can force values by elimination alone, with no search: if two elements can only be `1` or `2`, and two more overlap with different ranges, the ones with no other option get pinned down directly.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  in_domain A [1, 2].
  in_domain B [1, 2].
  in_domain C [2, 3].
  in_domain D [3, 4].
  all_dif [A, B, C, D].
  """)
```

## `in_domain` and `label`

`in_domain Var Values` restricts a var to a finite set, the same way `dif`/bounds do: a real constraint on the var, not a class with a `domain` method.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  in_domain X [a, b, c].
  findall X All (label X).
  """)
```

Posting `in_domain` twice narrows by intersection.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  in_domain Y [a, b, c, d].
  in_domain Y [c, d, e].
  findall Y All (label Y).
  """)
```

A domain narrowed down to exactly one value binds automatically, no `label` needed.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  in_domain Z [only_one].
  """)
```

`label` is the one place a bounded-but-still-open var actually becomes concrete. Inequalities and `in_domain` alone only ever narrow, they never enumerate. On an already-ground term, `label` is a no-op.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  label 5.
  = X 5.
  """)
```

On a genuinely open, bounded var, `label` enumerates every value as ordinary backtracking alternatives, cheapest first.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  >= X 3.
  <= X 5.
  label X.
  """)
```

A domain that's still open on at least one side has nothing finite to enumerate, so `label` fails rather than looping forever.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  >= X 3.
  label X.
  """)
```

### How `label` chooses witnesses

`label Term` is the explicit boundary between describing possible values and enumerating concrete ones. It follows this order:

1. If `Term` is already ground, succeed without changing it.
2. If it has an explicit finite domain, create one backtracking alternative per compatible member.
3. If it has finite integer bounds, enumerate the integers within those bounds.
4. If it is one side of an open `super` or slot relation, enumerate the distinct values found in real relation rows. A known value on the other side filters that search.
5. If it has object constraints, enumerate every compatible existing durable identity. Only real durable IDs are candidates.
6. Also find every compatible exact value class. For each one, ask the class's `witness` method for a concrete value. Numbers and lists provide their own witnesses; ordinary value classes normally construct one through their initialization protocol.
7. Propagate all existing `class`, `isa`, selected-method, slot, domain, bounds, and `dif` constraints through each candidate. Reject a candidate as soon as any constraint conflicts.

Each surviving candidate is an ordinary choicepoint, so `findall` can collect all of them. An `isa` constraint permits exact instances of that class and its descendants; a `class` constraint permits only that exact class. Intersecting `isa` constraints keep only common descendants.

Labeling a class variable in an open relation such as `class Object ExactClass` only chooses a class name. It does not construct `Object`. Label the object separately when a concrete object or value is required.

If AL cannot derive a finite domain, a relational row, a durable identity, or a value witness, `label` fails. It never guesses from an unbounded space.

## `either`: disjunctive constraints

`or` between two constraints is a real disjunction (`AL.Var.Bounds.either`), not the backtracking choicepoint of `;`. If one side gets refuted, the other is applied for real.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  = A 4.
  or (= A 5) (= B 7).
  """)
```

If neither side can be decided yet, `either` just stays parked, undetermined, rather than guessing.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  or (= A 5) (= B 6).
  """)
```

Both `A` and `B` are still open there. `either` is reactive too: post it before anything is ground, and it resolves once a refutation actually arrives.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  or (= A 5) (= B 7).
  = A 4.
  """)
```

Putting `findall`, `=`, `either`, and `label` together: every multiple of 3 or 5 below 20, with no duplicates. `15` is a multiple of both and would show up twice under a naive backtracking `;`. It doesn't here, because `either` resolves by elimination rather than by trying both branches.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  findall Candidate Candidates {
    < Candidate 20,
    > Candidate 0,
    or (= Candidate (* X 5)) (= Candidate (* Y 3)),
    label Candidate
  }.
  """)
```
